      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the probability that Terry and Mary both end up with the same color combination of candies. There are two possible scenarios where this can happen: both Terry and Mary pick two red candies, or both pick two blue candies.\n\nThe total number of ways to choose 2 candies out of 20 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{20}{2} = \\frac{20 \\times 19}{2} = 190 \\).\n\n**Scenario 1: Both pick two red candies.**\n\nThe number of ways to choose 2 red candies out of 10 is:\n\\[ \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2} = 45. \\]\n\n**Scenario 2: Both pick two blue candies.**\n\nThe number of ways to choose 2 blue candies out of 10 is:\n\\[ \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2} = 45. \\]\n\nThe total number of favorable outcomes (both pick two candies of the same color) is the sum of the two scenarios:\n\\[ 45 + 45 = 90. \\]\n\nThe probability of this happening is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes:\n\\[ \\frac{90}{190} = \\frac{9}{19}. \\]\n\nHere, 9 and 19 are relatively prime. Therefore, the probability is \\( \\frac{9}{19} \\), and the sum \\( m + n \\) is:\n\\[ 9 + 19 = 28. \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{28}. \\]",
